A module that refines another may bind an inherited abstract import (`import X : A`) only to a module that refines `A`: `A` itself, or a module that reaches `A` along its chain of `refines` clauses. The rule was not enforced: an alias import, a nested module, or another abstract import of a module that merely had members of the same names was accepted, and the refining module's inherited proofs, which assume `A`'s contracts, then proved `false`, or a compiled program divided by zero. Such a binding is now a resolution error: `a module (X) can only be replaced by a refinement of the original module`. A binding to `A` itself, to any module whose chain of `refines` clauses reaches `A`, to an export set of one, or to another abstract import of a refinement of `A`, is still accepted. The hidden option `--legacy-unchecked-import-binding` restores the old, unsound rule, for comparison and migration only. The reference manual said the binding must "adhere" to `A`, and its example (with copies in the FAQ and the online tutorial's source) bound an abstract import to a module that did not declare `refines`; that structural check was never implemented. The manual now states the nominal rule, and its example declares `module Implementation refines Interface`. A program that relied on the old acceptance migrates by adding that one clause.
